@node Building Timely Dataflows
@section Inputs, outputs and operators

This section tours dataflow construction: where data come from, how to
observe them, and the built-in operators that transform them.  One
assumption underlies all of it: @strong{every worker builds the same
dataflow}.  A worker's build function may read user input or a random
seed, but you should not: a sensible computation builds an identical
graph on every worker (@pxref{Running Timely Dataflows}).

@subsection Creating inputs

Almost every operator is defined from a source of data.  There are two
common sources.  @code{dtfl_to_stream} turns an iterator-like callback
into a stream, as in the first dataflow (@pxref{Timely Core Concepts}):
the callback emits records until it returns @code{false}.  An input
handle feeds a stream interactively: create it with
@code{dtfl_input_new}, add it to a scope with @code{dtfl_input_to_stream},
and send records with @code{dtfl_input_send} as the computation runs.  A
third source, @code{dtfl_op_builder_output} with no inputs, is a custom
source operator (@pxref{Running Timely Dataflows}); a fourth,
@code{dtfl_stream_capture_into} and the event-link replay, moves a
stream out of one dataflow and back into another.

@subsection Observing outputs

The simplest way to look at a stream is @code{dtfl_stream_inspect},
which runs a callback on each record.  Its larger sibling
@code{dtfl_stream_inspect_batch} hands the callback a whole batch of
records that share a timestamp, together with that timestamp
(@pxref{Timely Dataflow Model}); it is the efficient choice when a
program processes outputs in bulk or needs to see the time.
@code{dtfl_stream_capture_into} exfiltrates a stream as a sequence of
events (data arriving, and progress notifications) that another dataflow
can replay; libdtfl's own tests use capture to extract a stream and
assert it is correct.

@subsection Transforming data

Between a source and a sink a program computes.  libdtfl provides the
usual operators; the ones you reach for first are map, flat_map and
filter.

@strong{Map and flat_map.}  @code{dtfl_stream_flat_map} transforms each
record into zero or more output records of a chosen type.  (libdtfl
spells @code{map} as a flat_map that emits exactly one record; because a
record is a byte string, the output type is given explicitly.)  The
callback writes each output record through an emit handle:

@example
static void
plus_one(void *ctx, const uint64_t *time, const void *rec, size_t len,
    dtfl_emit *out)
@{
        unsigned char buf[8];
        dtfl_put_u64(buf, dtfl_get_u64(rec) + 1);
        dtfl_emit_record(out, buf, sizeof buf);
@}
/* dtfl_stream_flat_map(s, dtfl_type_u64(), plus_one, DTFL_NOCTX); */
@end example

To emit several records, call @code{dtfl_emit_record} more than once; to
emit none, call it zero times.  Mapping each @var{x} to @code{0 .. x},
say, is a flat_map whose callback emits a record per value in the range.

@strong{Filter.}  @code{dtfl_stream_filter} keeps the records whose
predicate returns @code{true}.  The predicate sees the record but, in
keeping with upstream, does not change it:

@example
static bool
is_even(void *ctx, const void *rec, size_t len)
@{
        return dtfl_get_u64(rec) % 2 == 0;
@}
/* dtfl_stream_filter(s, is_even, DTFL_NOCTX); */
@end example

The complete program in @file{doc/snippets/timely_ops.c} chains these:
stream 0 through 9, add one, keep the evens, and inspect.  It prints
@code{seen: 2}, @code{4}, @code{6}, @code{8}, @code{10}.

@subsection Splitting and combining

@code{dtfl_stream_partition} splits a stream into a fixed number of
output streams by a routing callback; it partitions only
@emph{logically} and moves no data between workers.
@code{dtfl_stream_concat} merges two streams, and
@code{dtfl_stream_concatenate} merges a whole array of them, undoing a
partition in one operator.  Both concat operators exchange no data and
copy no records.

@code{dtfl_stream_exchange} is the @emph{physical} partition: it routes
each record to the worker its routing callback selects, changing the
distribution of data without changing its contents.  You rarely call it
directly, since an operator that needs a particular distribution arranges
it itself; it earns its keep when two operators want the same
distribution of one stream, where a single @code{exchange} moves the
data once instead of twice.

Many more operators live in @code{timely_ops.h}; the next section shows
how to build your own when none of them fits.
